{"id":"plastic_deformation","compound_name":"Graphs::MPM::plastic_deformation","source_path":"plastic_deformation/plastic_deformation.json","category":"Shell","author":"Autodesk","description":"This graph utilizes the make_mpm_shell source to create a plastic deformation simulation.","documentation_file":"plastic_deformation_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Shell Deformation\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph utilizes the `make_mpm_shell` source to create a plastic deformation simulation.\n\n### Inputs and outputs\n\nThe inputs to this graph are the geometries that will be plastically deformed, and the output is the deformed mesh.\n\n### How it works\n\n- The result from the thumbnail can be obtained by simulating to frame 30 and rendering with Arnold using the physical sky.\n\n- The dummy_mpm_shell compound in this graph is simply creating and positioning the input mesh. You can visualize this geometry by toggling the diagnotic flag on the terminal. \n\n- Using `make_mpm_shell` instead of `make_mpm_cloth` provides plastic deformation functionality.\n\n- Enabling breaking will initiate the deformation process. Please note there is no `breaking post process` supported at the moment, which means the shell will not break apart similar to how a cloth would rip. However, it will plastically and permanently deform which is the first step towards breaking.\n\n- For a given `vibration_speed`, tweaking the `thickness` on the `make_mpm_shell` node, coupled with the `elastic_limit` in the breaking section will influence how pliable the shell is and its ability to spring back after deforming.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","mpm":"2.15.0.9","scatter":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":20,"connectio